For a uniform random variable distributed over \([a,b]\), \[\mathrm{Var}(X)=\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}\] Here, \[a=2,\qquad b=10\] \[\mathrm{Var}(X)=\frac{(10-2)^2}{12}
=\frac{64}{12}
=\frac{16}{3}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }\frac{16}{3}}\]

Given circuit is shown below. <img src="img/07b598e03b212d21.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> The equivalent resistance is \[Z_{\text{eq}}=(3\parallel 6)+1=2+1=3\,\Omega\] Hence, \[I=\frac{V}{Z_{\text{eq}}}
=\frac{3}{3}
=1\,\text{A}\] The power supplied by the \(3\,\text{V}\) source is \[P=VI=3\times1=3\,\text{W}\] \[\boxed{\text{D) 3.0}}\]

